# The ledger of `markdown`: what Go's goldmark (github.com/yuin/goldmark
# v1.8.7, with its `extension`, `ast`, `parser` and `renderer/html`
# packages), Rust's pulldown-cmark (0.13) and comrak (0.40), and Python's
# markdown-it-py (4.3.0) and Python-Markdown (3.11.3) let a program do with
# Markdown, one block per capability, and what Rebel answers. No standard
# library of the three reads Markdown.
#
# The lists are this machine's, 2026-11-17: `go doc` of goldmark's packages
# fetched into the scratch folder; the items of `cargo doc` of pulldown-cmark
# and comrak (structs, enums, functions, options' fields and constants); and
# `dir()` of `markdown_it`, `MarkdownIt`, `Token`, `SyntaxTreeNode`,
# `markdown` and `markdown.Markdown`.
#
# The tests hold every example of the CommonMark specification 0.31.2, 652,
# and GFM's 23 examples of tables, strikethrough, extended autolinks and task
# list items, each passing byte for byte. The differential, 2026-11-17,
# against commonmark.js 0.31.2 (the specification's reference in JavaScript,
# fetched into the scratch folder): 48,000 generated documents, 18,000 of
# inline fragments and 30,000 of block structure (indentation, tabs, every
# container marker), written alike but for 4, which the notes of md/read and
# md/lists name. 40 pathological inputs of 50,000 repetitions (nested
# brackets, emphasis, backticks, unclosed links, titles, raw HTML and
# comments, 50,000 block quotes, 3,000 nested lists, 200,000 lines) each read
# in 1.2 s or less and 200 MB or less.

capability md/read  CommonMark read whole: every block and inline of the specification
  heroes  markdown.parse, markdown.parse_with, markdown.Document
  go      Markdown, New, DefaultParser, parser.Parser, parser.NewParser, parser.Context, parser.NewContext, parser.DefaultBlockParsers, parser.DefaultInlineParsers, parser.DefaultParagraphTransformers, parser.LinkReferenceParagraphTransformer, parser.ProcessDelimiters
  rust    pulldown_cmark::Parser, pulldown_cmark::Parser::new, pulldown_cmark::Parser::new_ext, comrak::parse_document, comrak::Arena
  python  MarkdownIt, MarkdownIt.parse, markdown.Markdown, markdown.Markdown.reset
  note    CommonMark 0.31.2: its 652 examples pass. Two of the generated documents differ from commonmark.js the same way, and follow the specification: a paragraph that holds only link reference definitions, under a setext underline, is no paragraph, where commonmark.js writes `<p></p>`; a paragraph whose definitions are taken out is none elsewhere in both

capability md/html  a document written as HTML
  heroes  markdown.to_html, markdown.to_html_with, markdown.format_html
  go      Convert, Markdown.Convert, DefaultRenderer, renderer/html.NewRenderer, renderer/html.Renderer, renderer/html.Config, renderer/html.DefaultWriter, renderer/html.Writer, renderer/html.WriterConfig, renderer/html.WithWriter, renderer/html.WithXHTML
  rust    pulldown_cmark::html::push_html, comrak::markdown_to_html, comrak::format_html, comrak::RenderOptions
  python  MarkdownIt.render, markdown.markdown, markdown.Markdown.convert, markdown.markdownFromFile, markdown.Markdown.convertFile, markdown.Markdown.set_output_format, markdown.Markdown.output_formats
  note    as the reference renderer writes it, empty elements as `<br />`, `<hr />` and `<img ... />`, which goldmark writes with `WithXHTML`; a file is read with `read_file` or `fs` first

capability md/tree  the document as a tree a program walks
  heroes  markdown.node_of, markdown.children, markdown.parent, markdown.next_sibling, markdown.previous_sibling, markdown.descendants, markdown.Node, markdown.ROOT, markdown.Align, markdown.Task
  go      ast.Node, ast.Walk, ast.Walker, ast.WalkStatus, ast.NodeKind, ast.NodeType, ast.BaseNode, ast.BaseBlock, ast.BaseInline, ast.Document, ast.Paragraph, ast.Heading, ast.Blockquote, ast.List, ast.ListItem, ast.CodeBlock, ast.FencedCodeBlock, ast.HTMLBlock, ast.HTMLBlockType, ast.ThematicBreak, ast.TextBlock, ast.Text, ast.String, ast.CodeSpan, ast.Emphasis, ast.Link, ast.Image, ast.AutoLink, ast.AutoLinkType, ast.RawHTML, ast.LinkReferenceDefinition, ast.IsParagraph, ast.KindDocument, ast.KindParagraph, ast.KindHeading, ast.KindBlockquote, ast.KindList, ast.KindListItem, ast.KindCodeBlock, ast.KindFencedCodeBlock, ast.KindHTMLBlock, ast.KindThematicBreak, ast.KindTextBlock, ast.KindText, ast.KindString, ast.KindCodeSpan, ast.KindEmphasis, ast.KindLink, ast.KindImage, ast.KindAutoLink, ast.KindRawHTML, ast.KindLinkReferenceDefinition
  rust    pulldown_cmark::Event, pulldown_cmark::Tag, pulldown_cmark::TagEnd, pulldown_cmark::HeadingLevel, pulldown_cmark::CodeBlockKind, pulldown_cmark::LinkType, pulldown_cmark::Alignment, pulldown_cmark::CowStr, pulldown_cmark::InlineStr, comrak::nodes, comrak::arena_tree
  python  Token, Token.type, Token.tag, Token.children, Token.content, Token.info, Token.markup, Token.nesting, Token.level, Token.block, Token.hidden, SyntaxTreeNode, SyntaxTreeNode.walk, SyntaxTreeNode.parent, SyntaxTreeNode.children, SyntaxTreeNode.next_sibling, SyntaxTreeNode.previous_sibling, SyntaxTreeNode.siblings, SyntaxTreeNode.is_root, SyntaxTreeNode.is_nested, SyntaxTreeNode.pretty, SyntaxTreeNode.to_tokens
  note    nodes numbered in one list and linked to their parent, children and siblings, as comrak's arena and goldmark's tree hold them; pulldown-cmark answers a stream of events, which `descendants` gives in the same order. A `match` on `Node` answers every kind, so a program that misses one does not compile

capability md/change  the tree changed and written again
  heroes  markdown.add_child, markdown.insert_before, markdown.insert_after, markdown.remove, markdown.set_node
  go      ast.Node
  rust    comrak::arena_tree
  python  Token.attrSet, Token.attrPush, Token.attrJoin, Token.attrGet, Token.attrIndex, Token.attrItems, Token.attrs, Token.copy, Token.as_dict, Token.from_dict, Token.meta, SyntaxTreeNode.attrGet, SyntaxTreeNode.attrs
  note    goldmark's node methods (AppendChild, InsertBefore, RemoveChild and the rest) and comrak's arena do the same; an attribute map on a node, markdown-it's, has no counterpart: a node holds what its kind holds

